\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Precision zeros and Gaussian conditional independence}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{\phi\sim N(0,Q^{-1})} on #{\mathbb R^V}, where #{Q} is any
positive-definite self-adjoint operator on this counting space. Its
\newvocab{precision graph} has an edge #{\{a,b\}} for distinct
vertices exactly when #{\langle\delta_a,Q\delta_b\rangle\ne0}.
For pairwise disjoint subsets #{A,B,S}, say that #{S} separates #{A}
from #{B} if every graph path from #{A} to #{B} meets #{S}. Then
##{S\text{ separates }A\text{ from }B
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\phi_A\mathrel{\perp\mkern-9mu\perp}\phi_B\mid\phi_S.}
Here conditional independence means that the conditional joint law
factors into its two conditional marginals almost everywhere in
#{\phi_S}. The canonical Gaussian density kernels used below factor
at every conditioning value. For distinct vertices there is also the
pairwise equivalence
##{\langle\delta_a,Q\delta_b\rangle=0
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\phi_a\mathrel{\perp\mkern-9mu\perp}\phi_b
     \mid\phi_{V\setminus\{a,b\}}.}
No Laplacian sign condition on #{Q} is required. The counting-basis
matrices and the covariance-provider boundary are given in
\ref{fdip-00LW}.}

\proof{
\p{Order #{V} and use the counting basis, so the matrix entries are
#{Q_{ab}=\langle\delta_a,Q\delta_b\rangle}. First suppose
#{V=A\sqcup S\sqcup B}. Separation implies #{Q_{AB}=0}. With
#{\phi_S=s} fixed, the quadratic energy in #{a,b}
is the sum
##{\begin{aligned}
&\frac12a^TQ_{AA}a+a^TQ_{AS}s\\
&\qquad+\frac12b^TQ_{BB}b+b^TQ_{BS}s
  +\frac12s^TQ_{SS}s.
\end{aligned}}
The last term is constant. The two remaining positive Gaussian
factors normalize separately, proving independence at every #{s}.
Equivalently, the conditional precision is
#{\operatorname{diag}(Q_{AA},Q_{BB})}; its inverse has zero cross block.
The square completion is the coordinate case of
\ref{fdip-0060}.}

\p{For smaller disjoint #{A,B,S}, no connected component of the graph
on #{V\setminus S} meets both #{A} and #{B}. Assign its components
to two unions #{A',B'} with #{A\subseteq A'}, #{B\subseteq B'} and
#{A'\sqcup B'=V\setminus S}; components meeting neither may be
assigned arbitrarily. The preceding argument gives conditional
independence of #{\phi_{A'}} and #{\phi_{B'}} given #{s}. Taking
coordinate marginals gives the asserted independence of #{\phi_A}
and #{\phi_B}.}

\p{For the pairwise statement, fixing all other coordinates leaves
conditional precision and cross covariance
##{\begin{pmatrix}Q_{aa}&Q_{ab}\\Q_{ab}&Q_{bb}\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
\operatorname{Cov}(\phi_a,\phi_b\mid\phi_{V\setminus\{a,b\}})
 =\frac{-Q_{ab}}{Q_{aa}Q_{bb}-Q_{ab}^2}.}
The denominator is positive. Independence implies zero covariance,
and zero #{Q_{ab}} makes the conditional density a product. This
proves both directions.}
}

\p{A positive Gaussian law is \newvocab{Markov with respect to a graph}
#{\Gamma} if its precision is zero on all distinct nonedges of
#{\Gamma}; extra zeros on edges are allowed. The same proof gives
conditional independence across every separator of #{\Gamma}.
Conversely its separator condition implies these zeros by taking
#{S=V\setminus\{a,b\}} for each nonedge. This is the finite Gaussian
global Markov equivalence of
\citet{Proposition 1 and Corollary 1, p. 139; Proposition 2, pp. 140–141}{speed1986gaussian}.
The lattice harmonic-field statements of
\citet{Theorems 8.17 and 8.26, pp. 391 and 399}{friedli2017statistical}
concern their specified lattice precisions, not this arbitrary-precision
theorem.}

\p{The coordinate independence declaration, and the sense in which
it supplies ordinary independence rather than the graph argument,
is \ref{fdip-00LW}.}
